  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through early next week. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nTonight into Thursday, the ongoing ridge of high pressure will\nbegin to shift eastward, allowing for stronger return flow out\nof the SW to advect warmer air into the region. Highs on\nThursday will rebound, in fact soar above average for this time\nof the year, lifting into the low to mid 60s throughout the\nforecast area.\n\nAnother cold front will cross the forecast area early Friday\nmorning, and will again be mostly moisture starved. There is a\nchance for scattered rain showers along this boundary, however,\nrainfall amounts are likely to remain below a tenth of an inch,\nwith no improvement to drought conditions.\n\nHigh pressure returns through the weekend, keeping the pattern\ndry. Heading into early next week will be the next low pressure\nsystem, with a primary and secondary FROPA currently projected \nto cross our CWA sometime late Monday into Tuesday. That said, \ngiven the steep negative tilt of the upper level trough, \nmoisture will struggle to support precipitation along this \nboundary, and showers may once more yield little to no \nmeasurable precipitation: again, no improvement to drought \nconditions.\n\nOtherwise, the dry conditons is bad news for the ongoing water\nresources drought impacting the state. Much of Northern Maine, \nCentral Highlands and Eastern Downeast Maine are experiencing \nonly 50-75% of normal precipitation over the last 30 days. While\nin the last 14 days areawide is less than 90% of normal. \nOverall, 30 day Standardized Precipitation Index blend has the \nNorthern 2/3rd of the CWA in the Moderate to Severe Dryness \ncategory with portions of the Upper St. John Basin now dipping \ninto the Extreme Dryness category. \n\nThere is 38 USGS streamflow gages across the CWA which remain \ndismal, with 26 out of the 38 ranked Much Below Normal (<10th \npercentile). USGS Groundwater sites continue to show struggling \ngroundwater conditions, Clayton Lake is at Record Low (deep) \nconditions on record (35 year POR). Millinocket, Kenduskeag, \nHadley Lakes and Calais all reporting Below to Much Below Normal\nconditions. Fort Kent is very low end of Normal conditions \napproaching slipping down into Below Normal conditions. \nTherefore, the US Drought Monitor continues the entire CWA in at\nleast Moderate Drought with 58% of the CWA in Severe Drought, \nthis has the potential to worsen with continued precipitation \ndeficits.",
